{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's proposed distinguishing evidence (Trigger-Response Distribution / Poisson-vs-bursty delta) is not sufficient for the problem's evidentiary requirement. A hidden shared cause that triggers failures intermittently and at low rate can produce a failure process whose inter-arrival times are statistically indistinguishable from a homogeneous Poisson process over any finite observation window. In that case the 'bursty' signature is absent, so the proposed test would classify the failures as truly independent when they are in fact pseudo-uncorrelated. The target even acknowledges this gap as a countercondition (shared trigger mimicking Poisson) but still states the distinction 'is revealed' by the Poisson-vs-bursty delta, which the countercondition directly refutes.","counterexample":[{"implication":"SRC_001's Poisson-vs-bursty test would declare independence, but the failures are causally shared.","prediction":"Aggregate failure times appear Poisson with no observable burst synced to any recorded external event.","setup":"Two unrelated services fail at low rate; a hidden shared cause (a rare external API change) triggers both with probability p<<1 per exposure, with exposures widely spaced and randomized."}],"target":"5290a67690026a8076b457e25d604794751d978a09cc4c2ee0c37a10bc5616a2"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 fails the problem because its argument for decentralization rests on an unproved, non-quantified assertion about a 'Correlation Horizon' where coordination complexity becomes a source of correlated crashes. The problem specifically asks whether a large service with strictly more total internal redundancy is less resilient; SRC_002 never engages the strict-redundancy comparison. It assumes non-linear growth of consensus/synchronization complexity produces correlated failure, but provides no evidence threshold or failure model showing that this effect ever dominates the monolith's greater internal redundancy. Thus the conclusion ('inherently more resilient') does not follow: a monolith with strictly more replicas, erasure coding, and majority quorums can tolerate the same correlated failures the target invokes, and the target offers no evidence that the complexity-failure coupling exceeds the resilience gained from extra redundancy.","counterexample":[{"implication":"Strictly more internal redundancy can beat decentralization even with non-trivial coordination, contradicting 'inherently more resilient'.","prediction":"The decentralized set suffers total outage from a single shared library bug affecting all N operators; the monolith survives k simultaneous replica failures due to strict redundancy.","setup":"A single large service with N+4 redundant replicas across disjoint racks plus a simple, well-tested Paxos quorum layer; a decentralized network of N independent operators each running a single instance with minimal coordination."}],"target":"6bbda5906b607fcdb4d57a022cfe7c53a677933e6ce44a8447bf2e90ac9cefc6"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_003 correctly conditions the resilience claim on substrate diversity and identifies a concrete distinguishing failure mode: independent operators converging on the same stack create a hidden shared failure domain ('Shadow Monolith'). It supplies a falsifying countercondition (monolith's redundancy high enough to withstand single-substrate failure) and does not overclaim unconditional superiority. The answer engages both halves of the problem by arguing that apparent independence is insufficient without substrate diversity, and it provides a testable mechanism for distinguishing true independence from pseudo-uncorrelated failures.","target":"d5942180f92030190f02874abeddf2a83d97c0f42f42e2a51e5cfaa7a495e467"}]}